From 143e75d77780e91d3852f6b4089fdda490c6841b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 22:39:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] Run the sky view factor line sweep as a numba kernel by
 default

The line sweep of the horizons (sky view factor, horizon maps) is compiled
with numba and split between threads; the numpy code stays as the fallback
(engine='numpy', or when numba is missing, with a warning). Same algorithm,
same horizons (within 1e-12 rad in the tests, identical svf on Davos).

Davos DEM (747 x 500 cells, 72 directions): the svf takes 11 s on one core
and 6.6 s on two, against about 39 s and 21.5 s with numpy (topocalc: 18 s
on one core). The terrain step of the example goes from 25.6 s to 7.2 s on
two cores. The kernel compiles once on first use (about 10 s), then loads
from the numba cache.

With numba, CPU_cores sets the threads of the kernel and the directions run
one after the other; with numpy, it stays the number of processes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012FMkEYDUJxvxmd3SbUsJfP
